faq:
  - q: Can I come without bringing my child?
    a: Yes — a good share of these sessions run with the parent alone. The child does not always need to attend, and with younger children most of the change happens through what shifts at home. Where it would help for the child to join, we look at that together.
  - q: How is parent counselling different from family therapy?
    a: In family therapy the whole family is in the room and the work runs through the interaction patterns between members. In parent counselling the work is with the parent, focused on understanding the child's behaviour and building an approach that can actually be sustained at home. The two are sometimes used one after the other.
  - q: How many sessions does it take?
    a: Work focused on one specific issue often produces results within a few sessions. For sleep routines, boundary setting or sibling rivalry, three to six sessions is a common range. Where difficulties have run for a long time or affect several areas, the process can take longer.
  - q: Should both parents come?
    a: Coming together helps where possible, because two different approaches at home is one of the harder situations for a child. It is not a requirement though, and the work is productive with one parent. Where parents live separately, how the sessions will run is agreed at the outset.
  - q: Will I be blamed?
    a: No. The point is not to find fault but to make an unproductive loop visible. Most parents are already doing what they can; the difficulty usually lies not in the intention but in a method that does not land with this particular child. The session looks at why that is.
  - q: Is it suitable for my teenager?
    a: Yes — parent counselling is a common route during adolescence. Even when a young person does not want to attend sessions, work with the parent can change how communication runs at home. Balancing boundaries against room to breathe is the most frequent subject in this period.
