  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: Parent counselling is not therapy for the parent
    metin: The focus of the session is not your own therapeutic process but the relationship you have with your child. How your own upbringing shapes your reactions today will of course come up — but the work does not continue from there. If you come to feel that your own process needs its own space, [individual therapy](/en/services/individual-therapy.html) is a separate frame for that.
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: How does it differ from family therapy and child therapy?
    giris: 'All three work within the same family, but their focus differs:'
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Parent counselling
        metin: The parent is in the room. The focus is understanding the child's behaviour and building an approach that works at home. The child does not need to attend. It is usually the shortest of the three.
      - baslik: Child therapy
        metin: 'The child is in the room. The focus is the child''s own emotional world, and the work runs through age-appropriate means — play, drawing, story. Parent sessions accompany the process. More detail in [child psychologist](/en/blog/child-psychologist.html).'
      - baslik: Family therapy
        metin: The whole family is in the room. The focus is not one person but the interaction patterns between members. Where the difficulty shows up in the relationship rather than in one person, [family therapy](/en/services/family-therapy.html) is the better frame.
  - type: adimlar
    baslik: How the sessions run
    giris: 'The process usually follows this sequence:'
    adimlar:
      - baslik: First session
        metin: We get to know you and your child. What started when, what has been tried at home, what helped and what made things worse. This session is for assessment; you will not be handed a list of actions on the way out.
      - baslik: Making the pattern visible
        metin: We look at the moment before the behaviour and the moment after. An unnoticed order usually emerges — a loop that repeats at a particular hour, after a particular sentence. Seeing it is half the work.
      - baslik: Building a workable approach
        metin: What we look for is not the correct method from a book but the method you can sustain in your house. Advice that cannot be applied is useless, so suggestions take shape around your working hours, how many children you have, and how much energy you have left.
      - baslik: Trying it and reviewing
        metin: You try it between sessions, and then we look at it together. The part that did not work is not a failure to be embarrassed about — it is information, showing which assumption was wrong.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: turuncu
    baslik: When should you come?
    metin: If a difficulty has run for weeks, if what you have tried at home is not working, if the same argument repeats daily, or if you feel the relationship with your child growing tense, a session is worth having. There is no need to wait for something serious; questions brought early are usually answered more quickly.
  - type: metin
    baslik: 'In short: change usually starts at home'
    metin: |-
      A child's behaviour cannot be read apart from the web of relationships it sits in. This is why work carried out with the parent can change a child's daily life without the child being seen at all.

      In Famagusta we run parent counselling as part of our [child psychology](/en/services/child-psychology.html) work; for adolescence see [adolescent psychology](/en/services/adolescent-psychology.html), and where the whole family needs to be involved, [family therapy](/en/services/family-therapy.html).
